0 turnover> , largely confined to the 100-share index stocks , partly <ei31 reflected> the <ei32 flurry> of activity typical at the <ei33 close> of a two-week trading account and the <ei35 start> of a new account . But they <ei36 said> Friday 's focus on the top-tier stocks <ei38 telegraphed> active overseas <ei39 selling> and <ei40 showed> the broad-based fears over the status of the U.K. economy and Britain 's currency in the wake of the <ei42 upheaval> in Prime Minister Margaret Thatcher 's cabinet . A senior dealer with Warburg Securities <ei43 noted> British Gas , the most active blue-chip stock at 20 million shares traded , was <ei44 affected> by the political implications of Mr. Lawson 's <ei45 departure> and Mrs. Thatcher 's cabinet <ei46 shuffle> . He <ei47 attributed> the unusually high volume to broad-based <ei48 selling> on fears that the Thatcher government may be <ei49 in> turmoil and Britain 's Labor Party <ei50 positioned> to <ei51 regain> control of the government and <ei52 renew> <ei53 efforts> at <ei54 nationalization> . British Gas <ei55 shed> 8.5 pence a share to <ei56 close> at 185 pence ( $ 2.90 ) . Other dealers <ei57 added> that the blue-chip stocks in general were <ei58 hit> by profit-taking over concerns that London shares will <ei59 continue> <ei60 posting> <ei61 declines> and the uncertainty over sterling given that Mr. Lawson 's successor , John Major , had only been in the job one day . Besides British Gas , British Steel <ei63 skidded> 1.74 to 123.5 on turnover of 11 million shares . British Petroleum <ei64 fell> 5 to 286 on 14 million shares traded . Dealers <ei65 said> the multinational oil company was <ei66 pressured> by recent brokerage recommendations <ei67 urging> investors to <ei68 switch> into Shell Trading amp Transport . Shell <ei69 eased> 1 to 416 on turnover of 4.8 million shares . Among the other actively traded blue-chip issues , Imperial Chemical Industries <ei70 dropped> 11 to # 10.86 , Hanson <ei71 skidded> 9.5 to 200.5 , and British Telecom <ei72 fell> 10 to 250 . In Tokyo , stocks <ei73 closed> lower but above intraday lows in active trading . The Nikkei index was <ei74 pressured> down by <ei75 profit-taking> <ei76 triggered> by sharp <ei77 advances> made through this week and <ei79 fell> 151.20 points to 35527.29 . In early <ei80 trading> in Tokyo Monday , the Nikkei index <ei82 fell> 148.85 points to 35378.44 . On Friday , the Tokyo stock price index of first section issues was down 15.82 at 2681.76 . First-section volume was <ei84 estimated> at 1.3 billion shares , up from 886 million shares Thursday . An official at Wako Securities <ei86 said> brokerages ' excessive expectations about recent <ei88 advances> in Tokyu Group shares and real estate issues were <ei89 dashed> Friday . Dealers <ei91 placed> heavy buy orders in the morning to <ei93 start> the first trading day for November <ei96 transactions> . But they <ei97 failed> to <ei98 sell> these stocks to client investors , who were <ei99 cautious> about the sharp <ei100 gains> these issues <ei101 made> this week , the Wako official <ei103 said> . Fund managers <ei104 said> Friday 's <ei106 profittaking> was a natural <ei107 result> of the week 's \" abnormal fever \" in <ei109 buying> real estate , shipbuilding , steel and construction shares . Frankfurt prices <ei110 closed> lower again Friday , the fourth <ei112 decline> in the past five days and the <ei114 culmination> of a week that saw the DAX index <ei116 lose> 4 % . The DAX <ei117 dropped> 19.69 points Friday to 1462.93 . Traders <ei119 said> the continued <ei120 turbulence> in other markets , coupled with the <ei121 drop> in London following the Lawson <ei122 resignation> , were <ei123 responsible> . Traders <ei124 said> that selling pressure was n't enormous and that the DAX <ei125 dropped> Friday more on a lack of any substantial buying interest . They <ei127 said> contributing to the downward <ei128 drift> was the fact that many professional traders had <ei129 chosen> to <ei130 square> positions ahead of the weekend . \" It 's the whole uncertainty about what 's happening around us , \" said Valentin Von Korff , a trader at Credit Suisse First Boston in Frankfurt . \" If you take away the outside influences , the market itself looks very cheap . What 's happening here is n't justified by the fundamentals . \" Traders said the market remains extremely nervous because of the wild swings seen on the New York Stock Exchange last week . That 's leaving small investors with cold feet , they said , and prompting institutions to take a reserved stance on the sidelines as well , at least until the market in New York <ei133 settles> down somewhat . Elsewhere , share prices <ei134 closed> lower in Paris , Zurich , Amsterdam , Brussels and Stockholm , and were <ei135 mixed> in Milan . The British <ei136 shakeup> was widely <ei137 cited> for the <ei138 declines> . Share prices also <ei139 closed> lower in Sydney , Hong Kong , Singapore , Taipei , Manila , Wellington and Seoul . Concern about <ei140 declines> in other markets , especially New York , <ei141 caused> selling <ei142 pressure> . Here are price trends on the world 's major stock markets , as <ei143 calculated> by Morgan Stanley Capital International Perspective , Geneva . To make them directly comparable , each index is based on the close of 1969 equaling 100 . The percentage <ei144 change> is since year-end .", "gold": [["ei9", "causes", "ei6"], ["ei11", "causes", "ei6"], ["ei21", "causes", "ei23"], ["ei30", "causes", "ei32"], ["ei42", "causes", "ei40"], ["ei42", "causes", "ei39"], ["ei6", "causedby", "ei9"], ["ei6", "causedby", "ei11"], ["ei23", "causedby", "ei21"], ["ei32", "causedby", "ei30"], ["ei40", "causedby", "ei42"], ["ei39", "causedby", "ei42"]], "lang": "eng", "mentions": ["ei1", "ei3", "ei4", "ei5", "ei6", "ei9", "ei11", "ei12", "ei13", "ei14", "ei15", "ei16", "ei17", "ei18", "ei21", "ei22", "ei23", "ei25", "ei26", "ei29", "ei30", "ei31", "ei32", "ei33", "ei35"